2 0 obj
<</Parent 7 0 R/Next 3 0 R/Title(1.1 In previous projects you built applications that stored records and showed them back. Finding something in it meant knowing roughly what it was called: a LIKE '%word%' query, a filter, a list. In real life, this does not work if you want a useful search. Someone looking for "How do I make the container talk to the database" will never match a document titled "Service networking".)/Dest 430 0 R>>
endobj
3 0 obj
<</Parent 7 0 R/Next 4 0 R/Prev 2 0 R/Title(1.2 Two very different techniques answer that problem. The first is full-text search: the database reduces text to normalised lexemes, indexes them, and ranks documents by how well they match a query. It is exact, fast, and it is what most applications should have implemented instead of LIKE. The second is semantic search: a model converts text into a vector positioned so that texts about the same thing land close together. Comparing vectors then finds documents that mean the same thing without sharing a single word.)/Dest 431 0 R>>
endobj
4 0 obj
<</Parent 7 0 R/Next 5 0 R/Prev 3 0 R/Title(1.3 Put the second one in front of a language model that answers in a sentence instead of returning a list of links, and you have what's called a RAG (retrieval-augmented generation). RAG performs vector research, then uses the results to allow a model to make a precise answer.)/Dest 432 0 R>>
endobj
